WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.920
An ABG is a snapshot in time of everything
happening at the alveolar capillary

2
00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.480
interface and beyond it.

3
00:00:08.239 --> 00:00:11.520
This lesson takes you past the basics of
pH and PaCO2,

4
00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:15.739
and into the clinical reasoning that turns
six numbers into a management decision.

5
00:00:16.619 --> 00:00:20.819
We start with a systematic five-step
approach that you'll use for every ABG

6
00:00:20.819 --> 00:00:21.440
you encounter.

7
00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:22.739
Check the pH.

8
00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:24.559
Identify the primary disorder.

9
00:00:25.319 --> 00:00:25.979
Assess compensation.

10
00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:29.120
Evaluate whether compensation is
appropriate.

11
00:00:29.119 --> 00:00:32.020
Assess oxygenation and the A-a gradient.

12
00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:34.319
Every step, every time.

13
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:37.220
Compensation is where many students get
stuck.

14
00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.860
The lungs compensate for metabolic
disorders within minutes.

15
00:00:41.599 --> 00:00:44.860
The kidneys compensate for respiratory
disorders over three to five days.

16
00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:49.380
If the compensation doesn't match the
expected formula, you're looking

17
00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:52.240
at a mixed disorder, and we'll show you
exactly how

18
00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:53.139
to recognize that.

19
00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.220
We cover all four primary disorders in
depth.

20
00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:01.099
Respiratory acidosis, respiratory
alkalosis, metabolic acidosis,

21
00:01:01.259 --> 00:01:02.599
and metabolic alkalosis.

22
00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:05.040
For each one, we examine the causes,

23
00:01:05.319 --> 00:01:08.760
the expected compensatory response, and
the clinical context that makes

24
00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:09.500
it make sense.

25
00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.420
The A-a gradient deserves its own
attention.

26
00:01:13.099 --> 00:01:16.260
A normal gradient tells you the lungs are
transferring gas efficiently.

27
00:01:16.879 --> 00:01:19.959
A widened gradient tells you the problem
is inside the lungs,

28
00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:22.540
V/Q mismatch, diffusion impairment, or
shunt.

29
00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:26.139
This single calculation changes your
differential diagnosis.

30
00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:31.500
We finish with clinical case integration
using real ABG patterns from ARDS,

31
00:01:32.099 --> 00:01:35.199
COPD exacerbations, sepsis, and drug
overdose

32
00:01:35.199 --> 00:01:37.440
to show how the numbers connect to
management.

33
00:01:38.319 --> 00:01:40.379
An ABG isn't just a lab result.

34
00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:43.300
It's a conversation between the patient
and the clinician.

35
00:01:43.819 --> 00:01:45.120
Let's learn how to listen.

